namespace gismo {
/**

\page Python_nanobind_interface Python via nanobind

\gismo provides Python bindings via <a href="https://github.com/wjakob/nanobind">nanobind</a>, producing a package called \c pygismo. Nanobind is a lightweight binding library offering fast compile times and small binaries.

<h2>1. Overview</h2>

<h3>Key characteristics</h3>

- <b>Package name</b>: \c pygismo (PyPI: \c pygismo)
- <b>Build option</b>: \c -DGISMO_WITH_NANOBIND=ON
- <b>C++ standard</b>: Nanobind requires C++17; binding files are compiled separately from the C++14 \gismo library
- <b>Shared runtime</b>: All modules share one \c libnanobind-gismo.so runtime via \c NB_SHARED \c NB_DOMAIN \c gismo, enabling cross-module type visibility
- <b>Auto-discovery</b>: Binding files matching \c src/gsXxx/nanobind/*_nb.cpp are automatically found by CMake
- <b>Extensible</b>: Optional modules (e.g. \c gsModule) can add their own \c .so to the package, and can be published as independent PyPI wheels

<h3>Architecture</h3>

The \c pygismo package consists of multiple shared extension modules:

<table>
<tr><th>Module</th><th>Source</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>core.so</tt></td><td><tt>src/misc/gsNanoBind.cpp</tt></td><td>Package metadata (\c __version__, docstring)</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>gsCore.so</tt></td><td><tt>src/gsCore/nanobind/gsCore_nb.cpp</tt></td><td>Core classes (gsMultiPatch, gsBasis, ...)</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>auto-discovered</i></td><td><tt>src/gsXxx/nanobind/*_nb.cpp</tt></td><td>One \c .so per binding file</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>gsModule.so</tt></td><td><tt>optional/gsModule/src/nanobind/gsClass_nb.cpp</tt></td><td>Optional module bindings</td></tr>
</table>

All modules link against \c libgismo.so (the shared \gismo library) and \c libnanobind-gismo.so (the nanobind shared runtime). The \c __init__.py imports \c core and all auto-discovered modules with <tt>from .xxx import *</tt>.

<h3>File layout</h3>

~~~~
cmake/
  gsConfig.cmake         # nanobind find_package, nanobind-gismo runtime setup
  gsLibrary.cmake        # pygismo_core target + auto-discovery loop + pygismo_full
  gsInstall.cmake        # install rules (regular and SKBUILD wheel)
  gismoConfig.cmake.in   # exposes GISMO_WITH_NANOBIND, GISMO_PYGISMO_DIR
src/
  misc/gsNanoBind.cpp    # core.so (version, docstring)
  gsCore/nanobind/
    gsCore_nb.cpp        # gsCore.so (gsMultiPatch, gsBasis, ...)
  gsXxx/nanobind/
    gsXxx_nb.cpp         # auto-discovered binding files
optional/
  gsModule/
    CMakeLists.txt       # in-tree build (nanobind section at bottom)
    src/nanobind/
      gsClass_nb.cpp     # gsModule.so binding source
    nanobind/
      CMakeLists.txt     # standalone build against installed gismo
      pyproject.toml     # wheel metadata (pygismo-gsmodule)
      README.md          # standalone/wheel build instructions
wheel/
  pyproject.toml         # wheel metadata for pygismo (scikit-build-core)
VERSION                  # version string read by scikit-build-core
~~~~

<h2>2. Getting started</h2>

<h3>Installation from pip</h3>

When available on PyPI, install with:
~~~~{.sh}
pip install pygismo
~~~~

Optional module extensions (e.g. gsModule) are installed separately:
~~~~{.sh}
pip install pygismo-gsmodule
~~~~

<h3>Building from source</h3>

<b>Prerequisites</b>:
- CMake 3.17+
- Python 3.8+ with development headers
- nanobind (\c apt \c install \c nanobind-dev or \c pip \c install \c nanobind)

~~~~{.sh}
# Configure with nanobind enabled
cmake -B build -DGISMO_WITH_NANOBIND=ON -DGISMO_BUILD_LIB=ON

# Build all nanobind modules + the gismo library
cmake --build build --target pygismo_full gismo

# The package is in build/pygismo/
~~~~

<h3>Local builds</h3>

There are two ways to use pygismo locally: building a wheel or pointing Python at the CMake build directory.

<h4>Option A: Build and install a wheel (recommended)</h4>

This produces a self-contained, installable package:

~~~~{.sh}
# Build the wheel (scikit-build-core drives CMake internally)
pip wheel wheel/ -w dist/

# On Linux, vendor the shared libraries into the wheel
pip install patchelf  # needed by auditwheel
LD_LIBRARY_PATH=wheel/build/cp*-linux_*/lib \
  auditwheel repair dist/pygismo-*.whl -w dist/repaired/

# Install the repaired wheel
pip install dist/repaired/pygismo-*.whl

# Use it — no path manipulation needed
python3 -c "import pygismo; print(pygismo.__version__)"
~~~~

On macOS, use \c delocate instead of \c auditwheel:
~~~~{.sh}
pip install delocate
delocate-wheel -w dist/repaired/ dist/pygismo-*.whl
~~~~

<h4>Option B: Use the CMake build directory directly</h4>

After building with CMake, add the build directory to Python's path:

~~~~{.sh}
cmake -B build -DGISMO_WITH_NANOBIND=ON -DGISMO_BUILD_LIB=ON
cmake --build build --target pygismo_full gismo
~~~~

Then in Python:
~~~~{.py}
import sys
sys.path.insert(0, "/path/to/build")
import pygismo
~~~~

Or using an environment variable:
~~~~{.sh}
PYTHONPATH=/path/to/build python3 my_script.py
~~~~

If gismo is installed to a prefix:
~~~~{.sh}
cmake --install build --prefix /your/prefix
~~~~
~~~~{.py}
import sys
sys.path.insert(0, "/your/prefix/lib")
import pygismo
~~~~

<h3>Usage</h3>

~~~~{.py}
import pygismo

print(pygismo.__version__)

mp = pygismo.gsMultiPatch()
print(mp.nPatches())
~~~~

<h2>3. Adding bindings for a class in src/</h2>

This section explains how to expose a new \gismo class to Python via nanobind. The process is designed to be minimal: create one file, reconfigure CMake, done.

<h3>Step 1: Create the binding file</h3>

Create a file at \c src/gsXxx/nanobind/gsXxx_nb.cpp. The naming convention \c *_nb.cpp is required for auto-discovery.

Here is a complete template:

~~~~{.cpp}
#include <gsCore/gsConfig.h>

#ifdef GISMO_WITH_NANOBIND

#include <nanobind/nanobind.h>
#include <nanobind/stl/string.h>

#include <gismo.h>

namespace nb = nanobind;

NB_MODULE(gsXxx, m) {
  m.doc() = "G+Smo gsXxx module";

  using MyClass = gismo::gsMyClass<real_t>;
  nb::class_<MyClass>(m, "gsMyClass")
    .def(nb::init<>())
    .def("myMethod", &MyClass::myMethod)
    ;
}

#endif // GISMO_WITH_NANOBIND
~~~~

<b>Important conventions</b>:
- Always guard with <tt>\#ifdef GISMO_WITH_NANOBIND</tt> / <tt>\#endif</tt>
- The \c NB_MODULE name (first argument) becomes the \c .so name and the Python submodule name
- Use \c real_t and \c index_t directly (without \c gismo:: prefix, since \c index_t is a macro)
- Include nanobind headers only in this file (never in global \gismo headers)
- Include STL type casters as needed: \c nanobind/stl/string.h, \c nanobind/stl/vector.h, etc.

<h3>Step 2: Reconfigure CMake</h3>

~~~~{.sh}
cmake --build build   # reconfigures automatically due to GLOB
~~~~

The file is auto-discovered by this CMake glob in \c cmake/gsLibrary.cmake:
~~~~{.cmake}
file(GLOB _nb_binding_files "${gismo_SOURCE_DIR}/src/gs*/nanobind/*_nb.cpp")
~~~~

Each discovered file gets its own \c nanobind_add_module target, linked against \c gismo, with output placed in \c build/pygismo/.

<h3>Step 3: Build and test</h3>

~~~~{.sh}
cmake --build build --target pygismo_full
cd build
python3 -c "import pygismo; print(dir(pygismo))"
~~~~

The new module's exports are automatically imported via \c __init__.py (which contains <tt>from .gsXxx import *</tt>).

<h3>Reference: existing binding file</h3>

See \c src/gsCore/nanobind/gsCore_nb.cpp for a production example that binds \ref gsMultiPatch with constructors, methods, static casts for overloaded functions, return-value policies, and default arguments.

<h2>4. Adding a new optional module</h2>

Optional modules (stored in \c optional/) can provide their own nanobind bindings. The module \c gsModule serves as a complete template.

<h3>Step 1: Create the binding file</h3>

Create \c optional/gsMyModule/src/nanobind/gsMyClass_nb.cpp following the same template as in section 3.

~~~~{.cpp}
#include <gsCore/gsConfig.h>

#ifdef GISMO_WITH_NANOBIND

#include <nanobind/nanobind.h>
#include <gsMyModule/src/gsMyClass.h>

namespace nb = nanobind;

NB_MODULE(gsMyModule, m) {
  m.doc() = "G+Smo gsMyModule bindings";

  nb::class_<gismo::gsMyClass>(m, "gsMyClass")
    .def(nb::init<>())
    .def("doSomething", &gismo::gsMyClass::doSomething)
    ;
}

#endif // GISMO_WITH_NANOBIND
~~~~

<h3>Step 2: Add nanobind section to the module's CMakeLists.txt</h3>

At the bottom of \c optional/gsMyModule/CMakeLists.txt, add:

~~~~{.cmake}
if (GISMO_WITH_NANOBIND)
  nanobind_add_module(pygismo_gsMyModule
    NB_SHARED
    NB_DOMAIN gismo
    "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/src/nanobind/gsMyClass_nb.cpp"
  )
  target_link_libraries(pygismo_gsMyModule PRIVATE gismo)
  set_target_properties(pygismo_gsMyModule PROPERTIES
    OUTPUT_NAME "gsMyModule"
    LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY "${PYGISMO_PKG_DIR}"
  )
  file(APPEND "${PYGISMO_PKG_DIR}/__init__.py"
    "from .gsMyModule import *\n")

  set(PYGISMO_TARGETS ${PYGISMO_TARGETS} pygismo_gsMyModule
    CACHE INTERNAL "nanobind module targets")
endif()
~~~~

<h3>Step 3 (optional): Create standalone build infrastructure</h3>

For publishing the module as an independent wheel on PyPI, create \c optional/gsMyModule/nanobind/ with:
- \c CMakeLists.txt — standalone CMake build against an installed gismo prefix
- \c pyproject.toml — scikit-build-core wheel metadata

See \c optional/gsModule/nanobind/ for a complete working example.

<h3>Step 4: Build and test</h3>

~~~~{.sh}
cmake --build build --target pygismo_full
cd build
python3 -c "from pygismo import gsMyModule; print('OK')"
~~~~

<h2>5. Wheel packaging</h2>

The nanobind bindings can be packaged as Python wheels for distribution via PyPI.

<h3>Base package: pygismo</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Item</th><th>Location</th></tr>
<tr><td>Build config</td><td><tt>wheel/pyproject.toml</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>Build backend</td><td>scikit-build-core</td></tr>
<tr><td>Version source</td><td><tt>VERSION</tt> file (must match CMakeLists.txt)</td></tr>
<tr><td>CI workflow</td><td><tt>.github/workflows/build_wheels.yml</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>PyPI secret</td><td><tt>PYPI_TOKEN</tt></td></tr>
</table>

The build process:
1. scikit-build-core drives CMake with \c -DGISMO_WITH_NANOBIND=ON
2. CMake builds \c gismo + \c pygismo_full
3. \c cmake \c --install places extensions into \c pygismo/
4. \c wheel.exclude filters out headers, cmake files, and data
5. \c auditwheel \c repair (Linux) / \c delocate (macOS) vendors \c libgismo.so and \c libnanobind-gismo.so into the wheel

To build locally:
~~~~{.sh}
pip wheel wheel/ -w dist/
~~~~

<h3>Module package: pygismo-gsmodule</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Item</th><th>Location</th></tr>
<tr><td>Build config</td><td><tt>optional/gsModule/nanobind/pyproject.toml</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>CI workflow</td><td><tt>.github/workflows/build_wheels_gsmodule.yml</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>PyPI secret</td><td><tt>PYPI_GSMODULE_TOKEN</tt></td></tr>
</table>

Module wheels install their \c .so into \c site-packages/pygismo/ alongside the base package. The base package owns \c __init__.py; module wheels only add extension files. Users import modules with:

~~~~{.py}
from pygismo import gsModule
from pygismo.gsModule import submoduleClass
~~~~

<h3>Publishing a new module wheel</h3>

1. Copy \c optional/gsModule/nanobind/ as a template
2. Adjust \c CMakeLists.txt and \c pyproject.toml
3. Create a GitHub Actions workflow (see \c build_wheels_gsmodule.yml)
4. Add the PyPI token as a repository secret

<h2>6. Build system internals</h2>

This section documents the CMake machinery that makes the nanobind integration work.

<h3>cmake/gsConfig.cmake (lines 227-255)</h3>

Responsible for:
- Finding Python 3.8+ and nanobind (apt package or pip fallback)
- Setting \c PYGISMO_PKG_DIR (build-tree output directory)
- Creating the \c nanobind-gismo shared runtime via \c nanobind_build_library()
- Resetting \c PYGISMO_TARGETS cache on first configure
- Writing the initial \c __init__.py
- Linking \c nanobind-gismo to \c Python::Python (fixes \c -Wl,--no-undefined)
- Setting \c EXCLUDE_FROM_ALL \c OFF on \c nanobind-gismo

This block is guarded by <tt>if (NOT TARGET nanobind-gismo)</tt> so it runs only once even if \c gsConfig.cmake is included multiple times.

<h3>cmake/gsLibrary.cmake (lines 96-136)</h3>

Responsible for:
- Creating \c pygismo_core from \c src/misc/gsNanoBind.cpp
- Auto-discovering \c src/gs*/nanobind/*_nb.cpp binding files via \c file(GLOB)
- Creating a \c nanobind_add_module target for each discovered file
- Appending to \c __init__.py and \c PYGISMO_TARGETS
- Creating the \c pygismo_full convenience target

<h3>cmake/gsInstall.cmake (lines 168-200)</h3>

Responsible for:
- Installing \c nanobind-gismo to \c lib/ (regular install) or skipping it (wheel build — auditwheel vendors it)
- Installing all \c PYGISMO_TARGETS to \c lib/pygismo/ (regular) or \c pygismo/ (wheel)
- Generating and installing \c __init__.py from the target list
- For wheel builds (\c SKBUILD defined): installing \c libgismo.so alongside the extensions (skipped for regular installs)

<h3>cmake/gismoConfig.cmake.in</h3>

The installed CMake config file. Exposes:
- \c GISMO_WITH_NANOBIND — whether nanobind was enabled
- \c GISMO_PYGISMO_DIR — install path of the \c pygismo/ package
- Calls \c find_dependency(Python) before importing targets (needed because \c nanobind-gismo has a \c PUBLIC link to \c Python::Python)

<h3>Technical constraints</h3>

- <b>C++17</b>: Nanobind requires C++17. The \c nanobind_add_module() command sets this automatically. \gismo itself compiles at C++14.
- <b>Separate files</b>: Nanobind headers must never appear in global \gismo headers.
- <b>\c -Wl,--no-undefined</b>: \gismo sets this globally. The fix is linking \c nanobind-gismo against \c Python::Python.
- <b>NB_DOMAIN</b>: All modules must use <tt>NB_DOMAIN gismo</tt> so that nanobind's internal type tables are shared across modules.
- <b>nanobind 1.9.2 (apt)</b>: No \c stl.h umbrella header (use \c stl/string.h etc.), and \c eigen/dense.h conflicts with \gismo's custom Eigen namespace (\c gsEigen).

*/
}
